=======================================================
SQL NOTES: WINDOW FUNCTIONS
=======================================================

A window function performs a calculation across a set of rows that are
related to the current row -- without collapsing those rows into one
like aggregate functions do.

The key difference from GROUP BY aggregates:

    GROUP BY + aggregate:
    SELECT gender, AVG(salary) FROM users GROUP BY gender;
    -- Returns ONE row per gender. Individual rows are gone.

    Window function:
    SELECT name, gender, salary, AVG(salary) OVER(PARTITION BY gender)
    FROM users;
    -- Returns ALL rows. Each row also shows the avg for its gender group.
    -- Individual rows are preserved. The aggregate is attached alongside.

Think of it like this:
    Aggregate + GROUP BY  -- summarizes, collapses rows
    Window function       -- calculates alongside, keeps all rows

=======================================================
CORE SYNTAX
=======================================================

    function_name() OVER (
        PARTITION BY column    -- optional: divide rows into groups
        ORDER BY column        -- optional: define row order within group
        ROWS/RANGE BETWEEN ... -- optional: frame clause (covered later)
    )

    OVER()        -- this is what makes it a window function.
                     Without OVER(), COUNT() is a regular aggregate.
                     With OVER(), COUNT() becomes a window function.

    PARTITION BY  -- divides rows into groups (like GROUP BY but rows kept)
                     if omitted, the entire table is one partition

    ORDER BY      -- defines the order of rows within each partition
                     required for ranking and running total functions

