=======================================================
SECTION 1: RANKING FUNCTIONS
=======================================================

-------------------------------------------------------
1. ROW_NUMBER() -- unique sequential number per row
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS row_num
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | salary | row_num
        Frank   | 90000  | 1
        Diana   | 80000  | 2
        Alice   | 75000  | 3
        Eve     | 70000  | 4
        Charlie | 60000  | 5
        Henry   | 60000  | 6       <- same salary as Charlie, still different number
        Grace   | 55000  | 7
        Bob     | 50000  | 8

    ROW_NUMBER() always gives a UNIQUE number to every row.
    Even if two rows have the same salary, they get different numbers.
    The tie-breaking order is not guaranteed -- it is arbitrary for ties.

    Use when: you need a unique sequential number regardless of ties.
              e.g., pagination (give me rows 11-20).

-------------------------------------------------------
2. RANK() -- rank with gaps for ties
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | salary | rnk
        Frank   | 90000  | 1
        Diana   | 80000  | 2
        Alice   | 75000  | 3
        Eve     | 70000  | 4
        Charlie | 60000  | 5       <- tied at rank 5
        Henry   | 60000  | 5       <- tied at rank 5
        Grace   | 55000  | 7       <- gap: rank 6 is skipped
        Bob     | 50000  | 8

    RANK() gives the same rank to tied rows.
    It then SKIPS the next rank(s) to account for the tie.
    Charlie and Henry both get rank 5, next rank jumps to 7 (not 6).

    Use when: standard competition ranking.
              e.g., two students with the same score both get 2nd place,
              next student gets 4th place (like Olympic medals).

-------------------------------------------------------
3. DENSE_RANK() -- rank without gaps for ties
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS dense_rnk
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | salary | dense_rnk
        Frank   | 90000  | 1
        Diana   | 80000  | 2
        Alice   | 75000  | 3
        Eve     | 70000  | 4
        Charlie | 60000  | 5       <- tied at rank 5
        Henry   | 60000  | 5       <- tied at rank 5
        Grace   | 55000  | 6       <- NO gap: continues at 6
        Bob     | 50000  | 7

    DENSE_RANK() gives the same rank to tied rows.
    It does NOT skip ranks after a tie.
    Charlie and Henry both get rank 5, next rank is 6 (not 7).

    Use when: you want ranks without gaps.
              e.g., salary bands, product tiers.

-------------------------------------------------------
ROW_NUMBER vs RANK vs DENSE_RANK -- side by side
-------------------------------------------------------

    salary | ROW_NUMBER | RANK | DENSE_RANK
    ------------------------------------------
    90000  |     1      |   1  |     1
    80000  |     2      |   2  |     2
    75000  |     3      |   3  |     3
    70000  |     4      |   4  |     4
    60000  |     5      |   5  |     5
    60000  |     6      |   5  |     5    <- tie handled differently
    55000  |     7      |   7  |     6    <- gap vs no gap
    50000  |     8      |   8  |     7

    ROW_NUMBER -- always unique, no ties
    RANK       -- ties get same rank, gaps after tie
    DENSE_RANK -- ties get same rank, NO gaps after tie

-------------------------------------------------------
4. NTILE(n) -- divide rows into n equal buckets
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS quartile
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | salary | quartile
        Frank   | 90000  | 1           <- top 25%
        Diana   | 80000  | 1
        Alice   | 75000  | 2
        Eve     | 70000  | 2
        Charlie | 60000  | 3
        Henry   | 60000  | 3
        Grace   | 55000  | 4
        Bob     | 50000  | 4           <- bottom 25%

    NTILE(4) divides rows into 4 equal groups (quartiles).
    NTILE(10) would create deciles (10 groups).
    NTILE(100) would create percentiles (100 groups).

    If rows don't divide evenly, earlier buckets get one extra row.

    Use when: you need to segment users into performance tiers,
              salary bands, percentile groups.

-------------------------------------------------------
Ranking with PARTITION BY -- rank WITHIN each group
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, dept, salary,
           RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS dept_rank
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | dept    | salary | dept_rank
        Diana   | HR      | 80000  | 1          <- rank within HR only
        Alice   | HR      | 75000  | 2
        Henry   | HR      | 60000  | 3
        Frank   | Finance | 90000  | 1          <- rank resets for Finance
        Eve     | Finance | 70000  | 2
        Charlie | IT      | 60000  | 1          <- rank resets for IT
        Bob     | IT      | 50000  | 2
        Grace   | IT      | 55000  | 3

    PARTITION BY dept restarts the ranking for each department.
    Without PARTITION BY, ranking is across the entire table.

    Very common interview question:
    "Find the highest paid employee in each department."

    SELECT * FROM (
        SELECT name, dept, salary,
               RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk
        FROM users
    ) ranked
    WHERE rnk = 1;

    Result:
        name  | dept    | salary
        Diana | HR      | 80000
        Frank | Finance | 90000
        Charlie| IT     | 60000

=======================================================
SECTION 2: VALUE FUNCTIONS
=======================================================

-------------------------------------------------------
5. LAG() -- access value from a PREVIOUS row
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           LAG(salary) OVER (ORDER BY salary) AS prev_salary
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | salary | prev_salary
        Bob     | 50000  | NULL        <- no previous row
        Grace   | 55000  | 50000
        Charlie | 60000  | 55000
        Henry   | 60000  | 60000
        Eve     | 70000  | 60000
        Alice   | 75000  | 70000
        Diana   | 80000  | 75000
        Frank   | 90000  | 80000

    LAG(salary) returns the salary of the previous row in the defined order.
    First row has no previous row, so it returns NULL.

    Practical use -- show salary difference from previous row:
    SELECT name, salary,
           LAG(salary) OVER (ORDER BY salary) AS prev_salary,
           salary - LAG(salary) OVER (ORDER BY salary) AS salary_jump
    FROM users;

    LAG with offset and default:
    LAG(salary, 2, 0)
    -- 2: look 2 rows back (default is 1)
    -- 0: return 0 instead of NULL when there is no previous row

-------------------------------------------------------
6. LEAD() -- access value from a NEXT row
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           LEAD(salary) OVER (ORDER BY salary) AS next_salary
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | salary | next_salary
        Bob     | 50000  | 55000
        Grace   | 55000  | 60000
        Charlie | 60000  | 60000
        Henry   | 60000  | 70000
        Eve     | 70000  | 75000
        Alice   | 75000  | 80000
        Diana   | 80000  | 90000
        Frank   | 90000  | NULL        <- no next row

    LEAD(salary) returns the salary of the next row in the defined order.
    Last row has no next row, so it returns NULL.

    LAG vs LEAD:
        LAG()  -- looks BACKWARD (previous row)
        LEAD() -- looks FORWARD (next row)

    Practical use: compare each month's sales to next month's.
    Compare each employee's salary to the next higher salary.

-------------------------------------------------------
7. FIRST_VALUE() -- first value in the window partition
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, dept, salary,
           FIRST_VALUE(salary) OVER (
               PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC
           ) AS highest_in_dept
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | dept    | salary | highest_in_dept
        Diana   | HR      | 80000  | 80000
        Alice   | HR      | 75000  | 80000       <- always shows HR's highest
        Henry   | HR      | 60000  | 80000
        Frank   | Finance | 90000  | 90000
        Eve     | Finance | 70000  | 90000       <- always shows Finance's highest
        Charlie | IT      | 60000  | 60000
        Bob     | IT      | 50000  | 60000
        Grace   | IT      | 55000  | 60000

    FIRST_VALUE returns the first value in the ordered partition.
    With ORDER BY salary DESC, first value = highest salary in that dept.
    Every row in the partition sees the same first value.

-------------------------------------------------------
8. LAST_VALUE() -- last value in the window partition
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, dept, salary,
           LAST_VALUE(salary) OVER (
               PARTITION BY dept
               ORDER BY salary DESC
               ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING
           ) AS lowest_in_dept
    FROM users;

    IMPORTANT: LAST_VALUE requires an explicit frame clause.
    Without it, the default frame only goes up to the current row,
    so LAST_VALUE would just return the current row's value (useless).

    ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING
    -- tells MySQL: consider ALL rows in the partition, not just up to current row.

    Use FIRST_VALUE over LAST_VALUE when possible -- it is simpler.
    For the minimum value, use FIRST_VALUE with ORDER BY salary ASC instead.

=======================================================
SECTION 3: AGGREGATE WINDOW FUNCTIONS
=======================================================

Regular aggregate functions (SUM, AVG, COUNT, MIN, MAX) can ALL be
used as window functions by adding OVER().

-------------------------------------------------------
9. Running total with SUM() OVER()
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           SUM(salary) OVER (ORDER BY id) AS running_total
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | salary | running_total
        Alice   | 75000  | 75000
        Bob     | 50000  | 125000
        Charlie | 60000  | 185000
        Diana   | 80000  | 265000
        Eve     | 70000  | 335000
        Frank   | 90000  | 425000
        Grace   | 55000  | 480000
        Henry   | 60000  | 540000

    SUM() OVER (ORDER BY id) accumulates as it goes row by row.
    This is a running total -- very common in financial reports.

    Running total per department:
    SELECT name, dept, salary,
           SUM(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY id) AS dept_running_total
    FROM users;
    -- Running total restarts for each department.

-------------------------------------------------------
10. AVG() OVER() -- average alongside each row
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           AVG(salary) OVER () AS company_avg,
           salary - AVG(salary) OVER () AS diff_from_avg
    FROM users;

    OVER() with nothing inside means: entire table is one window.
    Every row sees the same company_avg value.
    diff_from_avg shows how much each person is above or below average.

    With PARTITION BY:
    SELECT name, dept, salary,
           AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) AS dept_avg
    FROM users;
    -- Each row shows the average for its own department.

-------------------------------------------------------
11. COUNT() OVER() -- count alongside each row
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, dept,
           COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) AS dept_headcount
    FROM users;

    Result:
        name    | dept    | dept_headcount
        Diana   | HR      | 3
        Alice   | HR      | 3
        Henry   | HR      | 3              <- all HR rows see 3
        Frank   | Finance | 2
        Eve     | Finance | 2
        Charlie | IT      | 3
        Bob     | IT      | 3
        Grace   | IT      | 3

    Every row knows how many people are in its department.
    Without window functions this would require a subquery or JOIN.

=======================================================
SECTION 4: FRAME CLAUSE
=======================================================

The frame clause defines WHICH rows within the partition are included
in the calculation for each row. It refines the window further.

    ROWS BETWEEN start AND end

    start / end options:
        UNBOUNDED PRECEDING  -- from the very first row of the partition
        N PRECEDING          -- N rows before current row
        CURRENT ROW          -- the current row itself
        N FOLLOWING          -- N rows after current row
        UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING  -- until the very last row of the partition

-------------------------------------------------------
Default frame when ORDER BY is used
-------------------------------------------------------

    RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW

    This means: from the first row of the partition up to the current row.
    This is why running totals work naturally with ORDER BY.
    This is also why LAST_VALUE needs an explicit frame -- its default
    frame stops at the current row, not the end of the partition.

-------------------------------------------------------
Moving average example (last 3 rows including current)
-------------------------------------------------------

    SELECT name, salary,
           AVG(salary) OVER (
               ORDER BY id
               ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
           ) AS moving_avg_3
    FROM users;

    For each row, averages the current row and the 2 rows before it.
    First two rows average fewer rows (only what is available).
    Useful in financial data to smooth out spikes.

=======================================================
WINDOW FUNCTIONS vs GROUP BY -- WHEN TO USE WHICH
=======================================================

    Use GROUP BY when:
        You want a summarized result with one row per group.
        You do not need the individual rows anymore.
        e.g., total salary by department.

    Use Window Functions when:
        You want the individual rows AND a calculated value alongside.
        You need rankings, running totals, or row comparisons.
        e.g., each employee's salary AND their department's total salary.

    -- GROUP BY (loses individual rows):
    SELECT dept, SUM(salary) AS total
    FROM users
    GROUP BY dept;
    -- 3 rows returned (one per dept). Names are gone.

    -- Window function (keeps individual rows):
    SELECT name, dept, salary,
           SUM(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) AS dept_total
    FROM users;
    -- 8 rows returned. Each row has name, salary, AND dept total.

=======================================================
QUICK REFERENCE
=======================================================

Function                        | Purpose
------------------------------------------------------------------------
ROW_NUMBER() OVER(ORDER BY ...) | Unique sequential number, no ties
RANK() OVER(ORDER BY ...)       | Rank with gaps after ties
DENSE_RANK() OVER(ORDER BY ...) | Rank without gaps after ties
NTILE(n) OVER(ORDER BY ...)     | Divide rows into n equal buckets
LAG(col, n, default)            | Value from n rows BEFORE current
LEAD(col, n, default)           | Value from n rows AFTER current
FIRST_VALUE(col) OVER(...)      | First value in partition window
LAST_VALUE(col) OVER(...)       | Last value (needs full frame clause)
SUM(col) OVER(ORDER BY ...)     | Running total
AVG(col) OVER(PARTITION BY ...) | Group average alongside each row
COUNT(*) OVER(PARTITION BY ...) | Group count alongside each row

Clause          | Purpose
------------------------------------------------------
OVER()          | Marks a function as a window function
PARTITION BY    | Divides rows into groups (like GROUP BY but rows kept)
ORDER BY        | Defines row order within each partition
ROWS BETWEEN    | Defines which rows are included in the frame

Common interview questions using window functions:
    1. Find the highest paid employee in each department
       -> RANK() OVER(PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC), filter WHERE rnk = 1
    2. Find the second highest salary overall
       -> DENSE_RANK() OVER(ORDER BY salary DESC), filter WHERE dense_rnk = 2
    3. Show running total of salary
       -> SUM(salary) OVER(ORDER BY id)
    4. Compare each employee salary to department average
       -> salary - AVG(salary) OVER(PARTITION BY dept)
    5. Find difference between current and previous row
       -> salary - LAG(salary) OVER(ORDER BY salary)

